course: dictionary
lesson: 3582
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one you need. Fibre is a good example. A fibre is a thin thread, like the ones in wool or string. But on a food label, fibre means something else. Look at the words around it to decide.
explain: «Eat plenty of fibre: whole grains, fruit and beans are high in fibre.» Words like eat, food and high in tell you this fibre is in food, not a thread. Your gut needs it to stay healthy, even though your body cannot digest it.
explain: «Carbon-fibre running blades spring back.» Carbon-fibre is a material made of threads, so here fibre means a material built from very thin threads. «Fibre optic» is different again: it means cables that carry internet signals as light. Each time, swap in your meaning and check it still makes sense.
fact: A fibre is a very thin thread, like those in wool, cotton, string, hair or paper.
fact: Fibre in food is the part of plants, like vegetables, fruit, beans and wholemeal bread, that your body does not digest.
fact: Natural fibres come from nature, like wool, cotton and silk. Man-made fibres, like nylon and polyester, are made in factories.
fact: «Every fibre of my being» means with all my heart.
fact: Fibre is also spelled «fiber» in American English.
exercise: «Eat plenty of fibre to keep your gut healthy.» What is fibre here? | A kind of glass used in phones | *The part of plant food that your body does not digest | Strength inside a person | Thin threads in a jumper
exercise: «Carbon-fibre running blades spring back.» What is carbon-fibre? | A type of running shoe | *A material made of very thin threads | A bird's nest | A plant that grows in water
exercise: Which phrase shows fibre means inner goodness and strength? | *Moral fibre | Natural fibres | High in fibre | Fibre optic
test: «Water climbs up a paper towel because it clings to the fibres.» What are the fibres? | Little glass tubes for light | Pieces of fruit | Dry patches of juice | *Very thin threads in the paper
test: Sam says fibre optic cables carry signals as sound. What is the correct idea? | Cables that carry signals as sound | *Cables that carry internet signals as light | Cables made from plant food | Cables that stop the internet
exam: «A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet, because food gives fibre, energy and many substances a pill does not contain.» What does fibre mean here? | *Part of plant food that your body needs but does not digest | A type of vitamin pill | Strength inside a person | A thin thread in clothes
